  "markscheme": {
    "html": "<section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"18a\"><p><strong>18a</strong></p><p></p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"F_{cp} = F_{e}\"></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{m_{e}v^{2}}{r} = \\frac{kQq}{r^{2}}\"></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"r = \\frac{ke^{2}}{m_{e}v^{2}}\"></span></p>\n<p></p></section><section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"18b\"><p><strong>18b</strong></p><p></p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"mvr = \\frac{3h}{2 \\pi}\"></span></p>\n<p>Substituting answer from question part (b):</p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"mv \\left ( \\frac{ke^{2}}{m_{e}v^{2}} \\right ) =  \\frac{3h}{2 \\pi}\"></span></p>\n<p>Simplifying and rearranging:</p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"v = \\frac{2 \\pi ke^{2}}{3h}\"></span></p>\n<p></p></section><section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"18c\"><p><strong>18c</strong></p><p></p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"v  = \\frac{2 \\pi \\times (8.99 \\times 10^{9}) \\times (1.6 \\times 10^{-19})^{2}}{3 \\times (6.63 \\times 10^{-34})} \\approx 7.3 \\times 10^{5}\"></span> m s<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"^{-1}\"></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"E_{k} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (9.11 \\times 10^{-31}) \\times (7.3 \\times 10^{5})^{2} \\approx 2.4 \\times 10^{-19}\"></span> J</p>\n<p></p></section><section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"18d\"><p><strong>18d</strong></p><p></p>\n<p>Energy of electron in second excited state: <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"E = - \\frac{13.6}{3^{2}} \\approx -1.51\"></span> eV</p>\n<p>Energy of electron in first excited state: <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"E = - \\frac{13.6}{2^{2}} = -3.4\"></span> eV</p>\n<p>Difference (energy of emitted photon): <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"- 1.51 - (-3.4) = 1.89\"></span> eV</p>\n<p>Converting energy into joules: <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"1.89 \\times (1.6 \\times 10^{-19}) \\approx 3 \\times 10^{-19}\"></span> J</p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\lambda = \\frac{hc}{E} = \\frac{(6.33 \\times 10^{-34}) \\times (3 \\times 10^{8})}{3 \\times 10^{-19}} \\approx 6.3 \\times 10^{-7}\"></span> m or <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"630\"></span> nm</p>\n<p></p></section><section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"18e\"><p><strong>18e</strong></p><p></p>\n<p>In the Rutherford model electrons orbit the nucleus; however this means that electrons accelerate (centripetal acceleration) and emit electromagnetic radiation.</p>\n<p>As a result, electrons would spiral into the nucleus.</p>\n<p>In the Bohr model, electrons have discrete energy levels and can only be in specific orbits, where <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"mvr = \\frac{nh}{2 \\pi}\"></span>.</p>\n<p></p></section>"
  },
  "paper": "paper_2",
  "question": {
    "html": "<p>An electron orbiting a hydrogen nucleus is in the second excited state. The orbital radius of the electron is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"r\"></span> and its speed is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"v\"></span>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>a. Show that <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"r = \\frac{ke^{2}}{m_{e}v^{2}}\"></span>.</p>\n<p>  [1]</p>\n<p>b. Show that <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"v = \\frac{2 \\pi ke^{2}}{3h}\"></span>.</p>\n<p>  [2]</p>\n<p>c. Find the kinetic energy of the electron.</p>\n<p>  [2]</p>\n<p>d. A photon is emitted when the electron transitions from the second excited state to the first excited state. Find the wavelength of the photon.</p>\n<p>  [3]</p>\n<p>e. The Rutherford model was revised by Bohr who introduced the following addition: <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"mvr = \\frac{nh}{2 \\pi}\"></span>. State and explain why this addition was introduced.</p>\n<p>  [3]</p>"
  },
